¶
'''venomosalivary''' (ven″ō-mō-sal′i-vā-ri), ''a.'' [Irreg. < ''venom'' + ''salivary''.] Venomous, as saliva; of or pertaining to venomous saliva.
{{smaller block|I find that it is even easy to see the ''venomosalivary'' duct [of the mosquito] from the outside, shining through the skin at the base of the head and neck in the undissected specimen.
{{right|''Amer. Nat.'', XXII. 886.}}}}

'''venomous''' (ven′um-us), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''venimous'', ''venemous''; < ME. ''venimous'', ''venymous'', < OF. *''venimous'', ''venimeux'', ''venemouse'', F. ''venimeux'', also (after L.) ''vénéneux'' = Pr. ''verenos'', ''verinos'', also ''venenos'' = Sp. Pg. ''venenoso'' = It. ''velenoso'', ''venenoso'', < LL. ''venenosus'', poisonous, venomous, < L. ''venenum'', poison, venom: see ''venom''. Cf. ''venenous'', ''venenose''.] '''1.''' Full of venom; noxious or hurtful by means or reason of venom; venenose; poisonous: as, a ''venomous'' reptile or insect; a ''venomous'' bite.
{{smaller block|It is alle deserte and fulle of Dragouns and grete Serpentes, and fulle of dyverse ''venymouse'' Bestes alle abouten.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 41.}}}}
{{smaller block|The biting of a Pike is ''venomous'', and hard to be cured.
{{right|''I. Walton'', Complete Angler, p. 132.}}}}
'''2.''' Hence, noxious; virulent; extremely hurtful or injurious; poisonous in any way.
{{smaller block|<poem>I ne telle of laxatyves no store,
For they ben ''venimous'', I wot it wel;
I hem defye, I love hem nevere a del.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Nun’s Priest’s Tale, l. 335.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy tears are salter than a younger man’s,
And ''venomous'' to thine eyes. ''Shak.'', Cor., iv. 1. 23.</poem>}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Venemous'' thorns, that are so sharp and keen,
Bear flowers, we see, full fresh and fair of hue.</poem>
{{right|''Wyatt'', That Pleasure is mixed with every Pain.}}}}

