{{smaller|veins; and there is a direct communication between the right and left auricles of the heart.—'''Venous calculus.''' Same as ''veinstone'', 2.—'''Venous canal''' (''ductus venosus''), a fetal vein passing from the point of bifurcation of the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava. It becomes obliterated soon after birth, and then remains as a fibrous cord.—'''Venous circulation''', the flow of blood through the veins. See ''circulation of the blood'', under ''circulation''.—'''Venous congestion''' or '''hyperemia''', engorgement of the veins of a part, due to obstruction of the venous circulation. Venous hyperemia is more strictly the engorgement of the subcutaneous veins, or superficial venous congestion.—'''Venous duct.''' See ''ductus venosus'', under ''ductus''.—'''Venous hemorrhage''', bleeding from a vein. It is distinguished from arterial hemorrhage by the darker color of the blood and by the fact that it occurs in a steady stream, and not in forcible jets, as when an artery is opened.—'''Venous hum.''' See ''hum''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Venous plexus.''' See ''plexus''.—'''Venous pulse''', a pulsation occurring in a vein, especially that which exists normally in the jugular veins.—'''Venous radicles''', the finest beginnings of the venous system, continuous with the capillaries. Sometimes erroneously written ''venous radicals''.—'''Venous sinus.''' (''a'') One of the various large veins formed in the substance of the dura mater. See the distinctive names under ''sinus''. (''b'') A natural dilatation of a vein, or a cavity into which two or more veins empty in common. In different cases such a sinus may correspond to the auricle of a heart, to a cavity communicating with a heart, as a caval vein, or to a cavity inclosing a heart, as the so-called pericardium of some invertebrates.}}

¶
'''venously''' (vē′nus-li), ''adv.'' In a venous manner; as respects the veins or venous circulation.
{{smaller block|The membranes of the brain were ''venously'' congested.
{{right|''Lancet'', 1890, I. 751.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
quantity of liquid for sampling; a liquid-vent or vent-faucet. (''d'') In ''molding'', one of the channels or passages by which the gases escape from the mold. (''e'') The flue or funnel of a chimney. (''f'') A crenelle or loophole in an embattled wall. ''Oxford Glossary.'' (''g'') In steam-boilers, the sectional area of the passage for gases, divided by the length of the same passage in feet. ''Webster.'' (''h'') In musical instruments of the wood wind group, a finger-hole. (''i'') The end of the intestine, especially in animals below mammals, in which the posterior orifice of the alimentary canal discharges the products of the urogenital organs as well as the refuse of digestion, as the anus of a bird or reptile; also, the anal pore of a fish, which, when distinct from the termination of the intestine, discharges only the milt or roe. See cut under ''Terebratulidæ.''—'''3.''' A slit or opening in a garment.
{{smaller block|Item, j. jakket of red felwet, the ''ventis'' bounde with red lether.
{{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 476.}}
The coller and the ''vente.'' ''Assembly of Ladies'', lxxvi.}}
'''4.''' An escape from confinement, as for something pent up; an outlet.
{{smaller block|<poem>My tears, like ruffling winds lock’d up in caves,
Do bustle for a ''vent.'' ''Ford'', Lover’s Melancholy, v. 1.</poem>
This is mischief without remedy, a stifling and obstructing evil that hath no ''vent'', no outlet, no passage through.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xxvii.}}}}
'''5.''' Utterance; expression; voice.
{{smaller block|<poem>Free ''vent'' of words love’s fire doth assuage.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 334.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|Madam, you seem to stifle your Resentment: You had better give it ''Vent.'' ''Congreve'', Way of the World, v. 13.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The poor little Jackdaw,
When the monks he saw,
Feebly gave ''vent'' to the ghost of a caw.</poem>
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 212.}}}}
'''6†.''' A discharge; an emission.
{{smaller block|<poem>Here on her breast
There is a ''vent'' of blood.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., v. 2. 352.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To give vent to''', to suffer to escape or break out; keep no longer pent up: as, ''to give vent to'' anger.—'''To serve the vent.''' See ''serve''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To take vent''', to become known; get abroad.}}
{{smaller block|Whereby the particular design ''took vent'' beforehand.
{{right|''Sir H. Wotton.''}}}}

¶
'''vent'''<sup>1</sup> (vent), ''v. t.'' [< ''vent''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' To let out at a vent; make an opening or outlet for; give passage to; emit; let pass.
{{smaller block|How camest thou to be the siege of this moon-calf? can he ''vent'' Trinculos? ''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 2. 111.}}
{{smaller block|He ''vented'' a sigh e’en now, I thought he would have blown up the church.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man out of his Humour, iii. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' To furnish with a vent; make a vent in.
{{smaller block|The gun is then ''vented.'' ''Ure'', Dict., IV. 82.}}
{{smaller block|It is usually necessary to ''vent'' the punch by a small hole.
{{right|''Jour. Franklin Inst.'', CXXII. 331.}}}}
'''3.''' To give utterance, expression, or publicity to; especially, to report; publish; promulgate; hence, to circulate.
{{smaller block|<poem>In his brain
. . . he hath strange places cramm’d
With observation, the which he ''vents''
In mangled forms.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 7. 41.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Let rash report run on; the breath that ''vents'' it
Will, like a bubble, break itself at last.</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', ’Tis Pity, iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|After many speeches to and fro, at last she was so full as she could not contain, but ''vented'' her revelations.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, I. 294.}}}}
{{smaller block|And when mens discontents grow ripe there seldom wants a plausible occasion to ''vent'' them.
{{right|''Stillingfleet'', Sermons, II. iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>As children of weak age
Lend life to the dumb stones
Whereon to ''vent'' their rage.</poem>
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Empedocles on Etna, i.}}}}
'''4.''' Reflexively, to free one’s self; relieve one’s self by giving vent to something.
{{smaller block|Adams frequently ''vented himself'' in ejaculations during their journey.
{{right|''Fielding'', Joseph Andrews, ii. 10.}}}}

'''vent<sup>2</sup>†''' (vent), ''n.'' [< OF. ''vent'', wind, air, breath, scent, smell, vapor, puff, = Sp. ''viento'' = Pg. It. ''vento'', < L. ''ventus'', wind, = E. ''wind'': see ''wind''<sup>2</sup>, and cf. ''vent''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', and ''vent''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' Scent; the odor left on the ground by which the track of game is followed in the chase.
{{smaller block|When my hound doth straine upon good ''vent''.
{{right|''Turberville''.}}}}
{{smaller block|Let me have war, say I; it exceeds peace as far as day does night; it’s spritely, waking, audible, and full of ''vent''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iv. 5. 238.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Vent'' is a technical term in hunting to express the scenting of the game by the hounds employed in the chase.
{{right|''Edinburgh Rev.'', CXXXVI. 176.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''hunting'', the act of taking breath or air.
